    "turn_text": "Oh man, it's it's almost like asking me what my life story is really. So I started at the Y when my mom and I moved at the North Dallas My aunt and uncle helped us move and we went to a new school and nobody could pick me up when school got out. And so at first, I was taking the bus home, walking to the house and going in, watching cartoons, you know, playing with toys, not doing my homework. And then we found out about the YMCA after school care program that was offered at my school. And it was, you know, it was affordable. And it was a good option and it gave my family the chance to finish their work and then come get me. And so they signed me up for it. And it really started a, you know, 30 year, you know, relationship with the Y to where, you know, my counselor at that camp, Derek Smith is still a good friend of mine to this day. And I went to summer camps at the Y. I came so often that they pretty much started letting me come for free to the Y to camps there. And as they called it a CIT, a counselor in brain, but I was really just there for free, you know, and getting some structured time. My first job was at the Y as a lifeguard and swim teacher, even though I'm not a great swimmer, but I was good enough to teach people how to do that. I came back and worked at the Y between college and the NFL when I needed to have a job. Basically, at every stage of my life, the Y was incredibly important to me. I always say, for a kid who's raised by a single mother in Dallas, where else, but I've had the chance to ride horses and go sailing and shoot a bow and arrow and be in the outdoors in the way that I was. And I still love being out there. I love hiking. People when they find the help about me, sometimes they're surprised. I was like, well, you don't know about my experience with the why. So it's really, for me, there's probably no institution that's been more important for my life than the why. And I proudly rep the why everywhere I go.",
