    "turn_idx": 79,
    "turn_text": "not exactly like a seat where everybody was thinking that it was going to be a winnable election. And then I was able to become the Democratic nominee and then ultimately won the election by about seven points. But to me, my time in running for Congress and working in Congress is an extension of the work that I was trying to do as a lawyer. which is just basically to create the conditions for people to succeed. You know, to me, I think it's, you know, we create opportunities or we can take them away, but we can't create outcomes. And so it's up to us as individuals if we're given that chance to take advantage of it. But if we don't give you that chance, then we're failing you. And I know from having grown up in our Dallas public schools, how many kids didn't get that chance that I got, either because they weren't an athlete or they didn't have some of the advantages that I had. And their potential is not just a loss for them, it's a loss for us. We would do a lot better as a society. as a city, as a country, if they had been able to fully realize themselves and do the things that I know they're capable of. And so to me, that's when I see everything to the lens of a kid who understands that they're before the grace of God go by. And so I realized that if a few different breaks here and there, I would be in a very different situation. raised by a single mother who's the public school teacher, not making a lot of money. It's not really thought of that someday you're going to be in Congress, you know,",
