    "turn_text": "just amazing how hard it is to have children in this country. And that's not saying that for me, I just mean, for my mom growing up, it was a real, real struggle. Child care was an enormous struggle, especially in those early years before you got to pre-K. And that is a gap there. before we can get our kids even into our schools that we're losing a lot of people and we're losing a lot of women in particular out of the workforce who are having to stay home or who are working too many jobs while doing their their jobs at home. And it's something that is holding us back overall as an economy, as a society. And so in the Build Back Better bill, which has been talked about, I think a lot, in ways that people probably don't understand and seems like there's numbers being used and all that. I try to talk about the policies in it. And the ones that I think are most important for families Number one is capping the cost of childcare. Now people are paying unbelievable amounts for childcare if they can afford it. But everybody is paying more for the most part. Middle class, lower class families are paying more than they can afford because they don't want to leave their kids somewhere that's not safe. And this has gotten actually worse during the pandemic with a lot of childcare centers closing. And so costs have even gone up. But we're going to save the average family almost $15,000 a year in childcare with",
